The '''2021 FIFA Club World Cup''' (officially known as the '''FIFA Club World Cup UAE 2021 presented by Alibaba Cloud''' for sponsorship reasons) was the 18th edition of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]], a [[FIFA]]-organised international club [[association football|football]] tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations, as well as the host nation's league champions. The tournament was held from 3 to 12 February 2022 in the [[United Arab Emirates]].<ref name="dates">{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/media-releases/update-on-fifa-club-world-cup-and-ofc-preliminary-competition-for-qatar-2022 |title=Update on FIFA Club World Cup and OFC preliminary competition for Qatar 2022 |work=FIFA |date=29 November 2021 |access-date=29 November 2021}}</ref>

Defending champions [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] did not qualify as they were eliminated in the quarter-finals of the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League]]. The eventual winners of that competition, [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], won the Club World Cup for the first time, beating [[Al Hilal SFC|Al-Hilal]] of Saudi Arabia 1–0 in the semi-finals before requiring [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]] to claim a 2–1 win over Brazilian side [[Sociedade Esportiva Palmeiras|Palmeiras]] in the [[2021 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]].

==Host appointment==
An expanded Club World Cup in China was planned to be held in June and July 2021.<ref>{{cite news |last=Harris |first=Rob |url=https://www.apnews.com/8602ada7064c4a14b02a19c502220cdc |title=FIFA wants 24-team Club World Cup in 2021 |website=APNews.com |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |date=13 March 2019 |access-date=16 March 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/who-we-are/news/fifa-council-unanimously-appoints-china-pr-as-hosts-of-new-club-world-cup-in-202 |title=FIFA Council unanimously appoints China PR as hosts of new Club World Cup in 2021 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=24 October 2019 |access-date=24 October 2019}}</ref> However, due to fixture congestion caused by the postponement of the [[2020 Summer Olympics]] and the [[Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on association football|impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on football]], the Tokyo Olympics, [[UEFA Euro 2020|UEFA European Championship]] and [[2021 Copa América|Copa América]] were postponed from mid-2020 to mid-2021. As a result, FIFA announced in March 2020 that they would postpone the expanded Club World Cup to later in 2021, 2022 or 2023,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/who-we-are/news/statement-from-the-fifa-president |title=Statement from the FIFA President |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=17 March 2020 |access-date=17 March 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/who-we-are/news/bureau-of-the-fifa-council-decisions-concerning-impact-of-covid-19 |title=Bureau of the FIFA Council decisions concerning impact of COVID-19 |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=18 March 2020 |access-date=18 March 2020}}</ref> before commencing it in [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup|2025]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Olley |first1=James |title=FIFA to launch new Club World Cup format with 32 teams in 2025 |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/fifa-club-world-cup/story/4832860/fifa-to-launch-new-club-world-cup-with-32-teams-in-2025 |access-date=16 December 2022 |work=[[ESPN]] |date=16 December 2022}}</ref>

On 4 December 2020, the [[FIFA Council]] announced that the Club World Cup, using the previous format, would be held in late 2021 and hosted by Japan.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/who-we-are/news/fifa-council-passes-landmark-reforms-for-female-players-and-coaches-agrees-furth |title=FIFA Council passes landmark reforms for female players and coaches, agrees further steps in COVID-19 response |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=4 December 2020 |access-date=4 December 2020}}</ref> However, on 8&nbsp;September 2021, the [[Japan Football Association]] dropped its commitment to host the tournament, owing to the possibility of restrictions on fan attendance due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Japan]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/news/update-on-fifa-club-world-cup-2021|title=Update on FIFA Club World Cup 2021|work=FIFA|access-date=10 September 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=8 September 2021|title=Asian Youth Games in China postponed to 2022 owing to virus; Japan drops 2021 Club World Cup|url=https://www.straitstimes.com/sport/asian-youth-games-in-china-postponed-to-2022-owing-to-virus-japan-drops-2021-club-world-cup|access-date=8 September 2021|website=The Straits Times}}</ref> Numerous countries expressed interest in hosting the tournament as bidding was reopened, including Brazil, Egypt, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, South Africa, and the United Arab Emirates.<ref>{{cite news |last=Ogden |first=Mark |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/fifa-club-world-cup/story/4488688/fifa-club-world-cup-south-africa-to-rival-qataruae-as-potential-hosts-sources |title=FIFA Club World Cup: South Africa to rival Qatar, UAE as potential hosts - sources |work=[[ESPN]] |date=2 October 2021 |access-date=20 October 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://extra.globo.com/esporte/rio-deve-ser-candidato-sede-do-mundial-de-clubes-de-2021-diz-secretario-estamos-nos-movimentando-25199889.html |title=Rio deve ser candidato a sede do Mundial de Clubes de 2021, diz secretário: 'Estamos nos movimentando' |trans-title=Rio should be a candidate to host the 2021 Club World Cup, says secretary: 'We are moving' |work=Globo |date=16 September 2021 |access-date=20 October 2021 |language=pt}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.winwin.com/%D9%83%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%A5%D8%AD%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7-%D9%85%D8%B5%D8%B1-3-%D8%AF%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A5%D9%81%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%B4%D8%AD%D8%A9-%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B6%D8%A7%D9%81%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%88%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%A9 |title=إحداها مصر.. 3 دول إفريقية مرشحة لاستضافة مونديال الأندية |trans-title=One of them is Egypt.. 3 African countries are candidates to host the Club World Cup |website=winwin.com |date=13 September 2021 |access-date=21 October 2021 |language=ar}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.winwin.com/%D9%83%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1/%D8%AE%D8%A7%D8%B5-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8-%D9%8A%D9%83%D8%B4%D9%81-%D9%85%D9%88%D9%82%D9%81%D9%87-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B6%D8%A7%D9%81%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%88%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%A9-2021 |title=خاص.. المغرب يكشف موقفه من استضافة مونديال الأندية 2021 |trans-title=Special.. Morocco reveals its position on hosting the 2021 FIFA Club World Cup |website=winwin.com |date=15 September 2021 |access-date=21 October 2021 |language=ar}}</ref> On 20 October 2021, the [[FIFA Council]] named the United Arab Emirates as the host of the tournament, and postponed the event from late 2021 to early 2022.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/organisation/fifa-council/media-releases/fifa-council-endorses-global-summit-to-discuss-the-future-of-football |title=FIFA Council endorses global summit to discuss the future of football |work=[[FIFA]] |date=20 October 2021 |access-date=20 October 2021}}</ref>

